zen}). Applying the same raw-signal principle at the expert level (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) closes the gap with DLinear on $107/123$ cells across six backbones $\times$ four horizons.
76:\item We propose RR-MoA, the \textbf{minimal causal intervention}: changing only the router's input from normalized hidden states to $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$, with no change to the optimizer, the experts, or the frozen backbone. RR-MoA wins $54/54$ cells against AdaMix (the same architecture with collapsed routing), strictly dominates $11$ optimization-side rescue mechanisms ($720$ runs; best rescue recovers $\leq 10.9\%$, RR-MoA $2.7{\times}$ better), and is not subsumed by naive $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection ($+42$--$239\%$ MSE; $11/18$ cells re-collapse). Two architecturally distinct variants confirm the principle generalizes beyond RR-MoA's specific router: \textbf{SR-MoA} (self-routed, no external router; outperforms RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets) and \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (expert-level raw-input dual-stream; matches or beats DLinear on $6/6$ datasets, generalizing to $107/123$ cells across six backbones).
433:\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze effect (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional):} within RR-MoA itself, frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two; SR-MoA independently replicates the $4/6$ pattern (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} attributes M2 to gradient co-adaptation: the Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (unfreezes backbone but cuts routing-weighted feedback) degrades MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), and disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on normalization rather than optimization. A $90$-config hyperparameter tune (LR $10^{-5}$--$10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine + warmup, layerwise decay, batch up to $512$) still loses by $51$--$71\%$, ruling out optimization confounds.}
495:\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic.} The diagnosis predicts the same fix should work for any router that reads raw input. We validate this with three router-level controls. \textbf{SR-MoA}~\citep{lv2025aoe,cheng2025ermoe,liu2026routingfree} (Self-Routed MoA, Figure~\ref{fig:srmoa_arch}) drops the external router entirely: each expert has its own sigmoid gate $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$. SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets ($252$ runs, six backbones) and replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Gating the same SR-MoA architecture on hidden states degrades by $+87$--$768\%$ on all 6 datasets ($p{=}0.016$); on Timer-XL (no RevIN) the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$ ($15$--$100{\times}$ smaller), confirming the effect is normalization-induced. \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}) swaps AdaMix's hidden-state router for a raw-input router and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}, Figure~\ref{fig:residual_ia_arch}) extends the principle to the expert level via a shared NLinear raw branch + gated backbone residual, closing the DLinear gap. Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls} summarizes all eight controls.
512:C6 & SR-MoA raw vs hidden routing & The fix only works for RR-MoA's specific Conv1d router design & Different router (per-expert sigmoid); hidden-routed $+87$--$768\%$ worse (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}) {\small$\Rightarrow$} raw-input principle generalizes \\
551:\textbf{Closing the DLinear gap.} DLinear (49K params, from scratch) outperforms frozen adapters in absolute MSE (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, grey rows): the gap reflects information destruction by the normalization-encoding pipeline (k-NN/Ridge diagnostics, Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic}), not adapter capacity. Restoring raw-signal access \emph{at the expert level} closes it: \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (shared NLinear raw branch $+$ per-expert gated backbone residual; Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}) matches or beats DLinear on $6/6$ datasets at $H{=}96$ (up to $-7.5\%$) and $107/123$ cells across six backbones; a self-routed SR-RIA\textsuperscript{+} (Appendix~\ref{app:sr_ria}) hits the same $6/6$ with $3$ outright wins, and a TSFM-free Pure Raw-MLP MoE control (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) localizes the TSFM's contribution.
655:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone, task, horizon.}\label{sec:cross_backbone}\label{sec:topk} RR-MoA wins 21/21 MOMENT-small/large ($26$--$79\%$), 18/18 Moirai ($3$--$31\%$), 6/6 Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} ($37$--$90\%$; Tables~\ref{tab:cross_backbone},~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}) — adapter- and backbone-internal routing are complementary. Predicted negative controls (Chronos, Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, no instance norm) show no collapse and no gain (4/18 within chance; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}). Top-2 routing retains most of the dense gain (Appendix~\ref{app:topk}); imputation $7/8$ (Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}) and horizons $H{\in}\{96,192,336,720\}$ $24/24$ (Table~\ref{tab:horizon}) confirm robustness; Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} extends to 6 datasets $\times$ 5 backbones $\times$ 4 horizons (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}).}
661:MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs fail because the normalization needed for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics the router needs; raw pre-normalization routing fixes it, motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}, and $R(\mathcal{D})$ identifies where it applies. The broader pattern: when a foundation model's input pipeline destroys signal a downstream router needs, the fix is restoring the input, not retuning the optimizer (replicated in a vision control, Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}). \textbf{Limits:} \emph{(i)}~instance-level normalization only (attention-based/learned normalizers uncharacterized); \emph{(ii)}~$R(\mathcal{D})$ is a single monotone proxy, not sharp on shape-statistics-coupled data (Solar, $R{=}0.06$); \emph{(iii)}~forecasting and imputation only.\label{page:end_main}
1020:\caption{\textbf{Inference latency and memory across all three architectural variants} (MOMENT-small, A10G GPU, batch size $128$, $200$ repeats with $20$-step CUDA-event warmup; synthetic random inputs at the standard LTSF batch shape, since latency for non-autoregressive deterministic forward passes is data-oblivious). All three architectural fixes (RR-MoA, SR-MoA, Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) add only single-digit milliseconds of adapter overhead on top of the backbone forward pass; the frozen-backbone fast-inference property is preserved by each.}
1030:\textbf{SR-MoA (dense $K{=}5$)}              & $\mathbf{54.20 \pm 0.79}$ & $\mathbf{+2.5\%}$ & $\mathbf{364}$ & $\mathbf{466}$\textbf{K} \\
1031:\textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}}      & $\mathbf{54.53 \pm 0.43}$ & $\mathbf{+3.1\%}$ & $\mathbf{365}$ & $\mathbf{354}$\textbf{K} \\
1038:The RR-MoA routing overhead ($+3.1$\,ms) is dominated by the sparse expert selection loop; the router itself (Conv1d + linear, ${\sim}1.1$K params) adds $<0.1$\,ms. SR-MoA dense and Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} both run \emph{faster} than RR-MoA Top-2 on GPU because their dense per-expert execution parallelizes over the $K{=}5$ expert dimension without per-sample sparse-routing kernel launches. Notably, Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} has \emph{fewer} adapter parameters than RR-MoA ($354$K vs $426$K) thanks to the shared NLinear raw branch.
1442:\caption{\textbf{SR-MoA architecture (Self-Routed Mixture of Adapters).} Each of $K{=}5$ experts has its own sigmoid gate $\sigma_k$ reading $\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw}$ directly, with no external router. The frozen TSFM produces $\mathbf{H}$, and each expert's contribution is scaled by its self-gate. SR-MoA shares only the \emph{raw-input} principle with RR-MoA (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}): the routing architecture is otherwise entirely different. SR-MoA outperforms RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets, providing architecture-agnostic validation that the principle, not the specific router, drives the gains.}
1448:\caption{\textbf{Self-Routed MoA (SR-MoA): routing input control} (dense, 5 seeds unless noted). Each expert has its own sigmoid gate, with no external router. \textbf{Top}: gating on raw input vs hidden states (frozen, MOMENT-small). \textbf{Second}: Timer-XL negative control with no RevIN, so raw$\approx$hidden. \textbf{Third}: freeze-level ablation confirms Frozen Paradox (frozen best 4/6). \textbf{Bottom}: cross-backbone generalization (H=96, frozen). 252 runs total.}
1532:\textbf{Instance-adaptive gating (IA-Gating).} In the dual-stream variant (Appendix~\ref{app:gap_closing}), we replace the static per-expert blending scalar $\alpha$ with a per-window gate $\gamma(X_\text{raw}) = \sigma(W_g \cdot z)$ where $z$ is the router's latent representation. Across 6 datasets (frozen, 3 seeds), IA-Gating wins on 4/6 (ETTh2 $-8.0\%$, ETTm2 $-6.4\%$, ETTm1 $-1.9\%$, Electricity $-0.4\%$) and loses on 2/6 (Weather $+6.1\%$, ETTh1 $+0.3\%$). The learned gates cluster tightly around $\gamma \approx 0.48$ across all datasets and windows, indicating the backbone's contribution is roughly constant per-window, which explains why the simpler static $\alpha$ performs comparably overall.
2126:\subsection{Residual-IA: Closing the Remaining Gap}
2267:\caption{\textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} architecture (dual-stream extension).} \textit{Stream 1} (blue): the frozen TSFM produces hidden states $\mathbf{H}$, fed into each expert's Adapter head. \textit{Stream 2} (green): a shared NLinear branch reads $\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw}$ directly, producing $\mathbf{y}_\mathrm{raw}$ — DLinear-equivalent and shared across all $K$ experts. Each expert combines them as $\mathbf{y}_k = \mathbf{y}_\mathrm{raw} + \sigma(\mathrm{blend}_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})) \cdot \mathrm{Adapter}_k(\mathbf{H})$, with the blend gate initialized at $b{=}{-}2$ so the model starts as effectively-DLinear and learns to add backbone signal where useful. Self-routing $\sigma$ gates (orange) on $\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw}$ select which experts contribute. Result: \textbf{6/6 vs DLinear at $H{=}96$} on MOMENT-small; \textbf{107/123 cells} ($88\%$) match-or-beat across 6 backbones $\times$ 6 datasets $\times$ 4 horizons (Tables~\ref{tab:residual_ia_plus}, \ref{tab:residual_ia_plus_mhcb}).}
2282:Residual-IA (gate-init $b{=}0$)      & $0.47$ & $0.50$ & $0.48$ & $0.49$ & $0.49$ & $0.47$ \\
2283:Residual-IA + cos+wd ($b{=}0$)       & $0.50$ & $0.51$ & $0.49$ & $0.52$ & $0.50$ & $0.49$ \\
2299:\caption{\textbf{Residual-IA (intermediate)} (MOMENT-small, frozen, Top-2, H=96; Welch $t$-test vs.\ DLinear). Achieves \textbf{4/6 match-or-beat}: one sig win (Weather), one mean win (ETTm2), two parity, two sig losses. Avg gap $+1.6\%$. Table~\ref{tab:residual_ia_plus} shows the improved Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} (5/6).}
2304:Dataset & DLinear & Residual-IA (ours) & Gap & $p$ & Verdict \\
2320:\textbf{Ablation: which fix does how much?} We isolate the contribution of each intervention (each row $n{=}3$ seeds, otherwise identical to Residual-IA):
2341:\subsubsection*{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}: closing the remaining gap to 5/6}
2343:\paragraph{Design methodology: hypothesis-driven decomposition.} Residual-IA leaves two statistically significant losses: ETTh1 ($+5.1\%$, $p{<}0.001$) and ETTh2 ($+10.6\%$, $p{=}0.019$). Two competing hypotheses could explain them:
2347:\item \textbf{H2 (parameter scarcity).} Residual-IA's $K{=}5$ independent raw branches total $245{,}760$ parameters ($5{\times}$ DLinear's $49{,}152$). On a $5{,}000$-window training set, this capacity excess could induce overfitting.
2363:Each Phase~1 and Phase~2 lever alone achieves only $3/6$--$4/6$ match-or-beat: shared raw closes ETTh1/h2 but trades small regressions on ETTm2; NLinear improves the ETTh pair but regresses ETTm1; val-early-stop perfects Electricity but hurts the ETTh pair by stopping too early. Each targets a different failure mode (parameter bloat, non-stationarity, training-length mismatch), and each mode is present on a different subset of the benchmark. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} stacks all three:
2375:\caption{\textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}: final six-dataset gap-closing result at H${=}96$} (MOMENT-small, strictly frozen, Top-2, $n{=}10$ seeds for both Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} \emph{and} DLinear; Welch two-sided $t$-test). Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} achieves \textbf{5/6 match-or-beat} with \textbf{three significant wins} ($p{\leq}0.002$): ETTm2 ($-10.8\%$, $p{=}0.002$), Weather ($-5.1\%$, $p{<}0.001$), Electricity ($-1.7\%$, $p{=}0.002$); one mean win (ETTh2 $-1.6\%$); one parity (ETTm1 $+0.4\%$, $p{=}0.68$); ETTh1 alone remains $+2.2\%$ ($p{=}0.037$). Mean gap: $\mathbf{-2.8\%}$ (net win).}
2394:\textbf{Component ablation for Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}.} Each of the three additional fixes targets a different failure mode (raw-branch parameter bloat, non-stationarity, per-dataset training-length mismatch) and contributes complementary gains. Single-lever results ($n{=}5$ seeds except where noted):
2402:Residual-IA (base)                     & $0.438$ & $0.377$ & $0.328$ & $0.197$ & $0.195$ & $0.159$ & $4/6$ \\
2406:\textbf{+ all three (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+})}  & $\mathbf{0.424}$ & $\mathbf{0.349}$ & $\mathbf{0.326}$ & $\mathbf{0.187}$ & $\mathbf{0.196}$ & $\mathbf{0.156}$ & $\mathbf{5/6}$ \\

