519:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Two distinct gains.} \emph{(Q1)} the best fixed adapter on the same RevIN-normalized hidden states still loses to RR-MoA by $26$--$79\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}) — residual shape heterogeneity survives normalization and rewards diverse experts; \emph{(Q2)} the signal-ratio $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ measures \emph{dispatch}-signal localization in the stripped statistics — why dense routing also benefits from raw access, and why Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) correctly does not improve. The Pure Raw-MLP MoE ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) shows the dataset-conditional choice between ``MoE with raw router'' and ``ensemble with raw input''.}
553:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Rawness vs.\ bypass and dose-response.} Replacing the router's raw input with RevIN-normalized input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$ while entropy stays high (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}): the router relies on the \emph{content} of the stripped statistics, not the bypass path. A dose-response interpolation $\mathbf{x}_\alpha = (1{-}\alpha)\mathbf{x}_\text{raw} + \alpha\,\text{RevIN}(\mathbf{x}_\text{raw})$ reveals a phase transition (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}; $150$ runs): MSE degrades by $+5.4\%$ for $\alpha{\leq}0.75$ but jumps to $+283\%$ at $\alpha{=}1$, with entropy held in $[1.43, 1.57]$ throughout. The threshold is quantitatively predicted at $\alpha^* = R(\mathcal{D})/(R(\mathcal{D})+1) \approx 0.40$ for the median benchmark (Appendix~\ref{app:prop2_depth}); a temporal-shuffle ablation confirms routing depends on $(M,\Sigma)$, not temporal ordering (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).}
556:\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$; RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$ and discards $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$. The mutual-information chain rule quantifies how much routing information lives in the discarded part.
560:Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
571:In the corner case where routing signal lives entirely in the stripped statistics ($E$ deterministic in $(M,\Sigma)$, $(M,\Sigma)\perp\!\!\!\perp S$; Part~(iii), Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}), $I(X;E){-}I(S;E)=H(E)$: RevIN destroys the entire routing distribution. We use the \emph{signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M)+\mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)]/\overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ as a tractable monotone proxy. The bound is empirically tight (CCA $\varepsilon\in[0.09,1.26]$ nats, KSG-validated~\citep{kraskov2004ksg}; gap $0.075$ nats, Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness}); learnable $\alpha$ converges to $\alpha{<}0.5$ on $30/30$ runs (Appendix~\ref{app:learnable_alpha}). $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts raw-routing benefit at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}), whereas $H(E\mid S)$ alone does not ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$). Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, $\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$) is correctly flagged a boundary case; Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier (SSR $+43\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).
655:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone, task, horizon.}\label{sec:cross_backbone}\label{sec:topk} RR-MoA wins 21/21 MOMENT-small/large ($26$--$79\%$), 18/18 Moirai ($3$--$31\%$), 6/6 Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} ($37$--$90\%$; Tables~\ref{tab:cross_backbone},~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}) — adapter- and backbone-internal routing are complementary. Predicted negative controls (Chronos, Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, no instance norm) show no collapse and no gain (4/18 within chance; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}). Top-2 routing retains most of the dense gain (Appendix~\ref{app:topk}); imputation $7/8$ (Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}) and horizons $H{\in}\{96,192,336,720\}$ $24/24$ (Table~\ref{tab:horizon}) confirm robustness; Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} extends to 6 datasets $\times$ 5 backbones $\times$ 4 horizons (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}).}
661:MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs fail because the normalization needed for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics the router needs; raw pre-normalization routing fixes it, motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}, and $R(\mathcal{D})$ identifies where it applies. The broader pattern: when a foundation model's input pipeline destroys signal a downstream router needs, the fix is restoring the input, not retuning the optimizer (replicated in a vision control, Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}). \textbf{Limits:} \emph{(i)}~instance-level normalization only (attention-based/learned normalizers uncharacterized); \emph{(ii)}~$R(\mathcal{D})$ is a single monotone proxy, not sharp on shape-statistics-coupled data (Solar, $R{=}0.06$); \emph{(iii)}~forecasting and imputation only.\label{page:end_main}
792:\newblock Reversible instance normalization for accurate time-series forecasting against distribution shift.
922:\newblock On the role of reversible instance normalization.
971:\paragraph{Backbone specifications.} MOMENT-small ($d{=}512$, 8 encoder blocks, internal RevIN); MOMENT-large ($d{=}1024$, 24 blocks, internal RevIN); Moirai ($d{=}384$, 6 blocks, LayerNorm only); Moirai-MoE ($d{=}384$, 6 blocks, LayerNorm only with sparse internal experts); Chronos ($d{=}512$, 6 encoder blocks, no instance normalization); Timer-XL ($d{=}1024$, 8 blocks, no instance normalization). Throughout, ``full fine-tuning'' means \emph{all} encoder blocks are unfrozen (8/8 for MOMENT-small, 24/24 for MOMENT-large, 6/6 for Moirai/Moirai-MoE/Chronos, 8/8 for Timer-XL).
1069:\textbf{Proof of Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}} (Routing Information Loss Under RevIN).
1072:Throughout, $X$ denotes a raw window, $(M,\Sigma)$ its per-window mean/scale, $S = (X-M)/\Sigma$ its RevIN-normalized shape (defined whenever $\Sigma > 0$; constant windows form a measure-zero exception that we exclude by convention), and $E$ a deterministic (possibly randomized) router assignment. On the support $\{\Sigma > 0\}$ the map $X \leftrightarrow (S, M, \Sigma)$ is a measurable bijection, so $I(X; Y) = I(S, M, \Sigma; Y)$ for any $Y$, and in particular $I(X; E) = I(S, M, \Sigma; E)$.
1095:\emph{Part (iii): Full-information corner.} Suppose (a)~the router depends entirely on the stripped statistics, $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M, \Sigma)$ (equivalently, $p(E,S \mid M,\Sigma) = p(E\mid M,\Sigma)\,p(S\mid M,\Sigma)$, a symmetric conditional independence), (b)~$(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ unconditionally, and (c)~$E$ is determined by $(M,\Sigma)$, so $H(E \mid M,\Sigma) = 0$. Conditions (a) and (b) yield the Markov chain $E \!-\! (M,\Sigma) \!-\! S$ in either direction; applying the data-processing inequality on this chain gives $I(S; E) \leq I(S; (M,\Sigma)) = 0$ (the second equality from (b)), so $I(S;E) = 0$. From (a) and (c), $I(X;E) = I((S,M,\Sigma); E) = I((M,\Sigma);E) + I(S;E\mid (M,\Sigma)) = H(E) + 0 = H(E)$. Hence $I(X;E) - I(S;E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the entire routing distribution.

