-{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the diagnosis matters more than the architectural label.} Existing diagnoses of MoE routing collapse split, broadly, between \emph{optimization-side} fixes that target training dynamics (load balancing, z-loss; effective when the router input is informative but under-optimized) and a \emph{representation-geometry} account in which routing learning itself encourages token clustering around expert centroids~\citep{chi2022representation}; architectural alternatives that decouple the router from the task objective~\citep{hua2025inputaware} or invert the token--expert paradigm~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice} sit alongside both. Our setting is upstream of all of these: an \emph{architectural inductive bias} (RevIN) strips the per-window statistics that carry the routing signal \emph{before training starts}. Optimization-side rescue mechanisms cannot recover what is structurally absent (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}), and representation-side reparameterizations presuppose information the input never carried. The mechanism decomposes into two distinct gains: \emph{(Q1)} the best fixed adapter on the same RevIN-normalized hidden states still loses to RR-MoA by $26$--$79\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}), so residual shape heterogeneity survives normalization and rewards diverse experts; \emph{(Q2)} the signal-ratio $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ measures \emph{dispatch}-signal localization in the stripped statistics, which is why dense routing also benefits from raw access and why Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) correctly does not improve. The Pure Raw-MLP MoE ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) makes this concrete: when the router goes to near-uniform on raw input, the architecture behaves as a raw-input ensemble, sufficient on Weather/ETTm2/Electricity (DLinear regime) but insufficient on ETT-temperature data where the TSFM contributes complementary nonlinear structure. The durable contribution is the diagnosis, not the architectural label; the choice between ``MoE with raw router