57	\begin{abstract}
58	Pioneering time series foundation models (TSFMs) such as MOMENT and TimesFM pair a frozen backbone with a task-specific head. Because downstream series mix heterogeneous regimes, the natural upgrade is a mixture-of-experts (MoE) head — a router over architecturally distinct experts, one per window. We identify a failure mode of this upgrade under instance normalization: any joint training of the adapter and backbone, even with only a single backbone layer receiving gradients, drives the router to a one-hot assignment, routing entropy to $0.000$, and starves all but one expert of gradients. We name this \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}: instance-normalization layers (e.g., RevIN) strip the per-window mean and variance the router needs. A $720$-run sweep across standard MoE rescue mechanisms (load balancing, z-loss, entropy regularization, ReLU routing, expert-choice) recovers at most $10.9\%$ MSE — $2.7\times$ worse than our causal intervention, because the failure is in the router's input, not its optimization. We formalize this via a mutual-information decomposition; the resulting signal-ratio statistic, computable before training, predicts dataset vulnerability (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$) and flags boundary cases. The diagnosis prescribes a minimal causal intervention: \emph{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters} (RR-MoA) routes on the raw, pre-normalization input. RR-MoA sets a new SOTA for adapter-based TSFM transfer under a strictly frozen backbone: $54/54$ wins on the primary backbone ($26$--$79\%$ MSE improvement over the best fixed adapter), strictly beats LoRA, TRACE, AdaMix, and full fine-tuning at $p{<}0.001$, and generalizes across four further backbones and an imputation task. Eight causal controls including a vision-modality replication isolate the cause to instance normalization, and predict a \emph{Frozen Paradox}: frozen RR-MoA beats full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$, dominated by the MoE pool's architectural advantage with a smaller within-architecture freeze effect ($\leq 13\%$) attributable to gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}). Applying the same raw-signal principle at the expert level (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) closes the gap with DLinear on $107/123$ cells across six backbones $\times$ four horizons.
